      {"question":"Which CV joint clicks when turning?","answer":"The outer joint is commonly responsible for repetitive clicking on a powered tight turn. Sound can travel, so inspection should confirm the side rather than relying only on the driver's impression."},
      {"question":"Can I replace only the CV boot?","answer":"Possibly if damage is found before contamination and wear occur. A joint that already clicks usually needs closer assessment and may require joint or shaft replacement."},
      {"question":"How quickly can a clicking CV joint fail?","answer":"There is no reliable distance. Load, contamination and wear differ, so prompt repair and avoiding full-lock acceleration are safer than trying to predict remaining life."},
      {"question":"Can a wheel bearing click on turns?","answer":"Bearings more often hum or rumble, but damage and hub-area faults can make varied noises. Bearing, brake, suspension and driveline checks should be performed together."}
